  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nAfter a dry evening with increasing clouds, rain showers begin \nto move in from southwest to northeast across eastern New York \nand western New England late tomorrow morning into tomorrow \nafternoon. These rain showers are remnants of post tropical \ncyclone Isaias, where we are expecting no flash flooding impacts\nin eastern New York and western New England as rainfall rates \nwill not be significant and a more steady and normal rainfall is\nexpected tomorrow. Additionally, most locations across eastern\nNew York and western New England have seen dry conditions the \nlast three to four days. Current forecast rainfall totals from \ntomorrow morning through Monday morning range between 0.5 inches\nto 1 inch. There could be periods of a moderate steady rainfall\nduring the afternoon hours, but forecast confidence is low at \nthis time on exactly where these steadier rain showers could \ndevelop. Nevertheless, these amounts will be over the 24 hour \nperiod ending primarily Sunday evening into early Monday morning\nfrom west to east. Dry conditions return for the day on Monday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAfter a brief break in the precipitation chances Monday, \nunsettled weather conditions return Tuesday as a low pressure \nsystem from the north slides into New York Tuesday morning. This\nweather system brings additional beneficial rainfall to eastern\nNew York and western New York with medium (greater than 50%) to\nhigh chances of rain showers throughout the day. Tuesday late \nafternoon into the overnight hours, a strong cold front is \ncurrently forecasted to move through and with enough \ningredients, there could be a few rumbles of thunder across \nlocations north of Albany during Tuesday afternoon ahead of the \nfront. For locations south of Albany, confidence is lower for \nrumbles of thunder as ingredients are not strongly favored to \nmaterialize at this forecast time. Latest probabilistic \nforecast guidance suggests for locations north of Albany have a \ngreater than 50% chance of more than 0.5 inches of rain in 24 \nhours from Tuesday morning through Wednesday morning. This \nbrings increasing confidence for rainfall and a dreary Tuesday \nis favored by the latest forecast. \n\nBelow normal temperatures return behind the cold front as a colder \nair mass above the surface returns for Wednesday through Thursday. \nHigh temperatures could range in the 50s and low 60s. Normal high \ntemperatures for the middle of October across the Greater Capital \nRegion range between 62 and 64 degrees, where Thursday high \ntemperatures could struggle to reach above 60. In the Mid-Hudson \nValley, highs are currently forecast to range in the low 60s.",
